    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d234">

                <pb facs="0439" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/1144</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Portugal</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Birch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Lisbon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-10-29T17:00:00+00:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">October 29, 1914, 5 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received October 30, 8 a.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>October 28, 4 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d234-f1"/> War not yet declared. State of
                    war does not exist. Portuguese commission conferring in England. Portuguese
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs this afternoon states House of Representatives will
                    be convoked Monday. Status remains the same as reported my recent
                    despatches.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Birch</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d234-f1" n="1" xml:id="d234-f1-content">
                <p>The telegram of October 28, 4 p.m., from the Secretary to the Minister read: Has
                    declaration of war against Germany, or other decree indicating that a state of
                    war exists, been issued by Potuguese Government? If so, report fully by
                    telegraph. Lansing.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
